cards.body:
Paragraph 9.1 requires every bank to **publish prominently on its website** its commitment to treating consumers fairly and how it will deliver it. Guidance lets the bank use a separate Treat Customers Fairly Charter or fold the commitments into its Customer Service Charter with enough prominence.

At Pelangi Utama, Nurul Aina's team rewrote the Charter last year:
- each commitment maps to one of the FTFC principles in paragraphs 10 to 16, as paragraph 9.4 requires;
- every promise names a measurable service, such as how complaints are acknowledged;
- branch staff were briefed so they could explain the Charter when customers asked.

The lesson: a Charter is not a marketing poster. Supervisors compare it with what actually happens in files and complaints.

FTFC paragraph 14.15 requires staff, representatives and agents to be **adequately trained and competent** to give quality advice, to understand **key features and critical terms**, to explain **risk and reward** and key obligations, and to give consumers time to read and consider. Paragraph 14.16 adds a process to **check periodically** that they remain competent, for example by asking customers whether products were explained accurately. Paragraph 13.3 requires **training, supervision and monitoring** so staff do not recklessly, negligently or deliberately mislead.

At Pelangi Utama, Daniel now:
- reviews a sample of each banker's advice files every month;
- reads the post-sale welcome-call feedback for complex products;
- pairs new bankers with a senior adviser until they pass an internal product assessment.

Staff who sell capital market products may face separate Securities Commission competence rules as well.

Schedule 11 of the FSA allows disclosure to an **investigating officer** authorised under written law for an investigation or prosecution. The MCIPD policy document attaches strict **conditions**, including:

- the request must be **specific**: the customer's name and identification number if known, the account or document required, the law under which the offence is believed to have been committed, and the officer's name and contact details;
- it must be **in writing** on the prescribed application forms;
- for the Royal Malaysia Police, it must be signed by an officer ranked **higher** than the investigating officer, who must be at least an **Inspector**; other agencies have their own signatory rules.

A phone call, however official it sounds, does not meet these conditions. Route every request through the team your bank designates.

In its publication notice, BNM said it had imposed an **administrative monetary penalty of RM3.4 million** on CIMB Bank Berhad for breaching the standards in the customer information policy document then in force, and offered a **compound of RM6.4 million** for offences of unauthorised disclosure of customer information to third parties under **section 133(1)** of the FSA. The incidents occurred in **2018**; the actions were taken in **2019**.

BNM noted mitigating factors: the bank **reported the incidents voluntarily** and took **immediate remedial steps** to close gaps and strengthen controls.

Lessons for every staff member:
- secrecy breaches can lead to **both** a criminal compound and an administrative penalty;
- a policy breach (weak controls) is sanctioned separately from the disclosure itself;
- fast, honest escalation counts in the bank's favour.
